After 12+ years in DevOps and fixing 200+ real-world Kubernetes incidents, I stopped looking for magic tools and built a mental model.

So now, anytime a pod misbehaves, I walk through this fast.

1) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗴𝗲𝘁 𝗽𝗼𝗱𝘀 -𝗼 𝘄𝗶𝗱𝗲

Which node is it on? What’s its status? Look for patterns across nodes, zones, or taints.

2) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗱𝗲𝘀𝗰𝗿𝗶𝗯𝗲 𝗽𝗼𝗱 <𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗲>

Read the event timeline. Crashes, mount issues, probe failures they all show up here before logs.

3) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗹𝗼𝗴𝘀 -𝗽 <𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗲>

Grab logs from the previous container. Most people miss this and stare at an empty terminal.

4) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗴𝗲𝘁 𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝘀 --𝘀𝗼𝗿𝘁-𝗯𝘆=.𝗺𝗲𝘁𝗮𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗮.𝗰𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝗧𝗶𝗺𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗺𝗽

What was the first domino to fall? Node pressure? PVC delay? CNI freakout? This command never lies.

5) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗴𝗲𝘁 𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗽𝗼𝗶𝗻𝘁𝘀 <𝘀𝘃𝗰>

Is the service even routing to this pod? I've debugged "app not responding" cases where the pod was fine, it just wasn't reachable.

6) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝘁𝗼𝗽 𝗽𝗼𝗱

CPU/memory pressure? One look here can expose silent OOMKills or runaway loops.

7) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗲𝘅𝗲𝗰 -𝗶𝘁 <𝗽𝗼𝗱> -- 𝘀𝗵

Go inside. Check logs, DNS, envs, mounts. 99% of the time, I find something unexpected here.

8) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗴𝗲𝘁 𝗱𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 <𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗲> -𝗼 𝘆𝗮𝗺𝗹

Am I running what I think I’m running? Check image tags, env vars, limits. Never assume.

9) 𝗸𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹 𝗿𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝗵𝗶𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗱𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 <𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗲>

Was this broken from the last deploy? Rollbacks are free if you’re paying attention.

This 9-step flow has saved me days of debugging.
Because debugging isn’t just about logs. It’s about visibility, sequence, and asking smarter questions.
